00:11:00,308 --> 00:11:05,093
1 plus offset is 4 because offset was 3 when

270
00:11:05,125 --> 00:11:06,956
we passed it to this nested function call.

271
00:11:07,614 --> 00:11:09,878
So we get 4 as an index we return here, and

272
00:11:09,878 --> 00:11:12,094
that of course is correct. 99 is at position

273
00:11:12,206 --> 00:11:17,296
0, 1, 2, 3, 4. 4 is correct. If we wouldn't

274
00:11:17,328 --> 00:11:19,271
add +1 here to the offset, we would have

275
00:11:19,271 --> 00:11:21,069
returned 3 instead, which would be

276
00:11:21,118 --> 00:11:21,680
incorrect.

277
00:11:22,860 --> 00:11:24,945
With all of that, if we save that and

278
00:11:24,977 --> 00:11:27,788
reload, we get 4 here for 99. But now let's

279
00:11:27,869 --> 00:11:30,050
also validate some other numbers. Let's

280
00:11:30,050 --> 00:11:32,396
check for 1, which should give us 0,

281
00:11:33,857 --> 00:11:34,247
and it does.

282
00:11:34,929 --> 00:11:37,396
Let's check for 5, which should give us 1.

283
00:11:39,052 --> 00:11:41,860
It does do that. Let's check 9, which should

284
00:11:41,941 --> 00:11:42,458
give us 2.

285
00:11:44,606 --> 00:11:45,301
Looking good to me.

286
00:11:45,915 --> 00:11:48,580
Let's check 13, which should give us 3.

287
00:11:50,286 --> 00:11:53,416
That works. We checked 99 already, so let's

288
00:11:53,416 --> 00:11:55,710
check 100, which should give us 5, and

289
00:11:55,743 --> 00:11:56,368
that's the case.

290
00:11:57,187 --> 00:11:59,289
So that's binary search with recursion.

291
00:12:00,060 --> 00:12:02,611
As always with the recursion, can be tricky

292
00:12:02,643 --> 00:12:05,131
to wrap your head around, but in general the

293
00:12:05,131 --> 00:12:06,832
steps here should be clear. The main

294
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:09,351
difference to the loop-based solution is

295
00:12:09,528 --> 00:12:11,919
that in the loop we just change the indexes

296
00:12:12,497 --> 00:12:14,551
and we then calculate different middle

297
00:12:14,583 --> 00:12:17,629
indexes Here in the recursive solution, we

298
00:12:17,790 --> 00:12:20,515
always calculate the same middle index and

299
00:12:20,611 --> 00:12:23,768
we only briefly change the indexes to call

300
00:12:24,041 --> 00:12:26,926
our new function again with a subset of the

301
00:12:26,926 --> 00:12:29,506
original array. So we really cut the array

302
00:12:29,651 --> 00:12:31,798
into slices for the different function

303
00:12:31,830 --> 00:12:34,122
calls. And because we're operating on

304
00:12:34,154 --> 00:12:36,927
different array slices, we also need to

305
00:12:37,023 --> 00:12:38,995
carry forward that offset.

306
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:41,976
Now that's just one possible implementation

307
00:12:42,072 --> 00:12:44,382
of that recursive approach here. You could

308
00:12:44,478 --> 00:12:47,758
also write binary search with recursion with

309
00:12:47,870 --> 00:12:50,249
different approaches where you just forward

310
00:12:50,346 --> 00:12:52,564
indexes to find_element and you slice your

311
00:12:52,677 --> 00:12:55,056
array up inside of each recursive call,

312
00:12:55,908 --> 00:12:58,014
but this approach works fine.

313
00:12:58,576 --> 00:13:00,939
Of course, we now have one big question.

314
00:13:01,454 --> 00:13:02,900
What's the time complexity of that?
